{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-97b433c5075daf87","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/_dev/GLB/stepwise-fifa-qa/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"739\" start=\"240\" end=\"289\" returned=\"50\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   240|\n   241|This applies to every discrete unit the loop emits: per-service, per-context, per-ADR,\n   242|per-unit, per-diagram, per-tab.\n   243|\n   244|**Timestamps come from a real clock read.** `started_at` and `completed_at` MUST be\n   245|actual timestamps at the moment of writing — never round numbers back-filled at the end\n   246|to describe what the run \"would have\" done. A `completed_at` that postdates the file's\n   247|own mtime is a fabricated tracker and fails §2.1 check 5.\n   248|\n   249|**Schema variants.** A skill picks ONE of the three shapes below based on its work model\n   250|and uses it consistently for the whole run. The common envelope (`skill`, `session_id`,\n   251|`status`, `started_at`, `completed_at`) is identical across variants.\n   252|\n   253|- **List shape (default — shown above)** — uses `total`, `completed`, `items[]`. Suits\n   254|  skills that produce many discrete artifacts (e.g. `implementing-code` writing source\n   255|  files; `planning-code-tasks` tracking task entries).\n   256|- **Section shape** — uses `skeleton_written` (bool) + `sections{}` map. Suits skills\n   257|  that produce a single multi-section spec file from large unstructured inputs. See\n   258|  **Section 10** for the full protocol (skeleton-first + one-section-per-write +\n   259|  source extraction + CONTINUE re-entry).\n   260|- **Hybrid shape** — carries BOTH: `skeleton_written` + `sections{}` for the manifest,\n   261|  AND `total` / `completed` / `items[]` for the per-item files produced alongside it.\n   262|  Suits skills that emit a sectioned manifest *and* a fan-out of per-entry artifacts\n   263|  (e.g. `secreview-static-scanning`: `sections{}` tracks the SCAN-MANIFEST while\n   264|  `items[]` grows one entry per finding). See §10.1.\n   265|\n   266|**A hybrid tracker MUST say so.** State the variant in the FIRST ACTION callout —\n   267|\"write `_progress.json` using the **hybrid** variant (per §10.1)\" — so a reader can tell\n   268|a deliberate hybrid from a skill that drifted between two shapes. Carrying both field\n   269|groups *without* that declaration is the drift failure §2.1 check 5 catches:\n   270|`establishing-architecture-foundation` (fixed 2026-08-09) incremented list-shape counters\n   271|in one phase and wrote `sections{}` in the next, leaving no coherent denominator.\n   272|\n   273|Both halves of a hybrid follow their own rules: §2.a seeds `total` for the `items[]` half,\n   274|§10 governs the `sections{}` half. `secreview-memory-synthesis` and the `pentest-*` family\n   275|are the reference implementations.\n   276|\n   277|### Skill-Level Enforcement (FIRST ACTION / LAST ACTION)\n   278|\n   279|Every skill MUST include these two inline callouts — agents do not reliably follow\n   280|protocol-only references for session bookend writes:\n   281|\n   282|**At session start** (after folder creation, before any other file write):\n   283|\n   284|> **FIRST ACTION — MANDATORY:** Write `_progress.json` to the output folder before any other file write.\n   285|> This prevents the orchestrator from sending SIGINT.\n   286|\n   287|Include the minimal JSON schema inline in the skill so the agent can write immediately\n   288|without reading this file first. Use `\"session_id\": \"initializing\"` as placeholder.\n   289|\n</file>","isError":false}}
