{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-852823bd0c453a95","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/_dev/GLB/stepwise-fifa-qa/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"739\" start=\"360\" end=\"459\" returned=\"100\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   360|  Record the gap as a visible pending item / open question / assumption in the artifact\n   361|  and (when a blocker) escalate per §8 Deviation taxonomy. Marking a step COMPLETED with\n   362|  a known, silent gap is a protocol violation.\n   363|\n   364|**Skill-Level Enforcement.** Generative skills SHOULD include a one-line inline callout\n   365|at their finalization step so the agent triggers this without reading the protocol first:\n   366|\n   367|> **PRE-COMPLETION CHECK — MANDATORY:** Before the LAST ACTION completion write, self-validate\n   368|> the artifact against §2.1 (input-contract completeness · no unresolved placeholders ·\n   369|> context-pack grounding · format conformance · tracker integrity). Repair or surface gaps;\n   370|> never complete silently. A `COMPLETED` tracker still showing `total: 0` fails check 5.\n   371|\n   372|This gate is the counterpart to the FIRST/LAST ACTION callouts in §2: FIRST ACTION opens\n   373|the step, §2.1 qualifies the output, LAST ACTION closes it.\n   374|\n   375|---\n   376|\n   377|\n   378|---\n   379|\n   380|### 10.5 Tool Discipline (Hard Rules)\n   381|\n   382|These rules exist because each one has been observed as a failure mode. Violations defeat the protocol even when the prose discipline appears to be followed.\n   383|\n   384|- **Skeleton write: use `Write`.** Single tool call, single file creation. Do NOT compose the skeleton via `Bash` heredoc.\n   385|- **Section population: use `Edit`.** Targeted find-and-replace, one section per call. The `Edit` tool handles unicode natively and validates the prior content matches what you expect.\n   386|- **NEVER use `Bash` + `sed` / `awk` / `python3 <<EOF` / `cat >` to mutate SPEC_FILE.** These are bulk-rewrite paths disguised as targeted operations. They read the whole file into memory, transform it, and write it back — the exact pattern Phase B is designed to prevent. They also bypass `Edit`'s prior-content validation, so a bug in the script silently corrupts the spec.\n   387|- **If `Edit` fails on a section, fix the `Edit` call.** Provide more surrounding context to make `old_string` unique. Do NOT switch to `Bash` / `python3` as a workaround — that path has been observed to cascade: agent hits one snag, then rewrites all remaining sections in a single script, violating one-section-per-write.\n   388|- **NEVER compose multiple sections in memory before writing.**\n   389|- **NEVER emit more than one section per Write/Edit call.**\n   390|- **NEVER re-read raw sources during Phase B.** Read `_extractions/*.md` files, or SPEC_FILE for declared cross-section state.\n   391|- **Prefer ASCII punctuation in authored content.** Emit ASCII equivalents for *decorative* non-ASCII glyphs the model tends to insert: `-` or ` - ` for em/en dashes (`—`/`–`), `->` for `→`, straight quotes `\"` / `'` for smart quotes (`“ ” ‘ ’`), `...` for `…`, `-`/`*` for `•`, `x` for `×`, plain space for non-breaking space. This keeps English-output specs on the fast, safe targeted-`Edit` path by avoiding the §10.5.1 non-ASCII fallback (which forces full-file replace). **This rule removes gratuitous glyphs only — it NEVER changes the output language or strips *required* non-ASCII.** Accented and localized characters in non-English output (`á é í ó ú ñ ü ç ã …`) are content, not decoration: preserve them exactly and let §10.5.1 handle the run. Do not transliterate them to \"fix\" the fallback.\n   392|\n   393|If a section's content genuinely will not fit a single `Edit` call (extreme size), split that one section into sub-edits — but every sub-edit is still an `Edit` call against SPEC_FILE, never a Bash mutation.\n   394|\n   395|---\n   396|\n   397|#### 10.5.1 Non-ASCII Content Fallback (TEMPORARY — runtime workaround)\n   398|\n   399|**Status:** TEMPORARY. Remove this fallback once the runtime's targeted-edit operation supports UTF-8 reliably.\n   400|\n   401|**Why this exists.** Some runtimes' targeted-edit operations have been observed to fail with `'ascii' codec can't encode character ...` errors when the `new_string` contains non-ASCII codepoints (accented vowels in Spanish / French / Portuguese / German, em dashes, smart quotes, etc.). The same failure mode has been observed to **destroy the target file** (the tool truncates the file before catching the encode exception, so a failed edit zeroes out the prior content). Until the runtime is fixed, targeted-edit cannot be trusted for SPEC_FILEs that may contain non-ASCII text.\n   402|\n   403|**Detection (at Phase B entry).** If ANY of the following is true, apply this fallback for **every** section write in the run:\n   404|- The detected output language (per Step 2) is not English.\n   405|- Any input source or `_extractions/*.md` file contains a character with codepoint > 127.\n   406|- Detection is unreliable / ambiguous — default to this fallback. The cost is bounded.\n   407|\n   408|**Per-section flow under fallback:**\n   409|\n   410|1. **Read** SPEC_FILE from disk — this is the current state (skeleton + sections populated so far).\n   411|2. **Generate** this section's body in working memory using the inputs from Phase B step 1.\n   412|3. **Compose** the full file payload in working memory:\n   413|   - header / metadata block (verbatim from disk)\n   414|   - all previously populated sections (verbatim from disk)\n   415|   - this section, freshly populated\n   416|   - remaining stubs (verbatim from disk)\n   417|4. **Write** the composed payload via the full-file replace operation (`Write` in Claude Code, `replace` in other harnesses). One call, full file content.\n   418|5. **Update** `_progress.json`: set `sections.{section_name}: complete`.\n   419|6. **Drop** everything from working memory before the next section. SPEC_FILE on disk is the source of truth; do not carry composed content forward.\n   420|\n   421|The per-section discipline is unchanged: one section populated per inference call, working memory bounded, prior section bodies not held in context. What changes is the **tool** (full-file replace, not targeted-edit) and the **payload size** (the whole file rather than one section's diff).\n   422|\n   423|**Tradeoffs:**\n   424|- Per-call output grows from ~2K tokens (first section) to ~30K tokens (last section). Cumulative ~5× the targeted-edit path. Still well within gateway limits — nowhere near the 1M-token failure mode Section 10 was designed to prevent.\n   425|- Full-file replace is **atomic**: a failed write leaves the prior file intact. This is *safer* than the destructive failure mode of the broken targeted-edit operation.\n   426|- No `old_string` validation. Mitigation: the agent reads SPEC_FILE immediately before composing the payload (step 1), so it rebuilds from disk truth, not stale working memory. Skipping step 1 is a violation — the read is what replaces validation.\n   427|\n   428|**Hard rules under fallback (from §10.5, still in force):** one section per inference call; never `Bash` + `sed` / `awk` / `python3 <<EOF` / `cat >` to mutate SPEC_FILE (the full-file replace operation is allowed, shell-based mutations are not); never compose multiple sections in memory before the write.\n   429|\n   430|**When the runtime issue is resolved:** delete this subsection and return to the targeted-edit path defined in 10.4 and 10.5. The runtime fix is a two-line change: (1) UTF-8 encoding in the edit tool's write path, (2) atomic-write semantics so failed edits leave the prior file intact.\n   431|\n   432|---\n   433|\n   434|#### 10.5.2 Safe-Write Protocol (mandatory defensive backup for SPEC files)\n   435|\n   436|**Why this exists.** Even with the discipline in §10.5 and the fallback in §10.5.1, a single bad targeted-edit call has been observed to truncate a SPEC file to 0 bytes mid-run (authored work wiped). The Safe-Write Protocol adds a `.bak` snapshot and a destructive-edit refusal gate so a single bad call cannot destroy authored work.\n   437|\n   438|**Applies to:** any skill writing to `RESEARCH-SPEC-*.md`, `PLAN-SPEC-*.md`, `IMPL-STATE-*.md`, `REVIEW-SPEC-*.md`, or any other SPEC-shaped artifact the skill declares.\n   439|\n   440|**Rule 1 — Defensive backup before any large edit:**\n   441|\n   442|Whenever you must edit a SPEC file larger than 20 KB AND the edit changes more than 30% of the file's lines:\n   443|\n   444|1. Compute: `lines_to_change` (new content vs current).\n   445|2. If `lines_to_change > 0.30 * total_lines AND file_size_kb > 20`:\n   446|   - `cp {target_file} {target_file}.bak.{ISO_TIMESTAMP}` via a single Bash call.\n   447|   - Proceed with the edit.\n   448|   - On success (post-edit file is non-empty AND > 50% of pre-edit size): delete the `.bak` file.\n   449|   - On failure or suspected truncation (post-edit file is < 50% of pre-edit size): restore from `.bak`, then emit a structured event:\n   450|     ```json\n   451|     {\"event\":\"safe_write_restore\",\"file\":\"<path>\",\"reason\":\"post_edit_too_small\",\"pre_size\":<N>,\"post_size\":<M>}\n   452|     ```\n   453|\n   454|**Rule 2 — Never `replace_all=true` on SPEC files.** Reinforces §10.5 \"Section population: use `Edit`.\" Use targeted, anchored Edits with non-empty `old_string`. If a full rewrite is genuinely needed, write to a new file first, then `mv` atomically.\n   455|\n   456|**Rule 3 — Refuse destructive `replace` calls (empty / too-short `old_string`):**\n   457|\n   458|Before any `replace` / `Edit` whose `old_string` is empty (`\"\"`) or shorter than 10 characters: REFUSE the operation. Emit:\n   459|\n</file>","isError":false}}
